{
  "dom_id": "step-q334564",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}-(Δx)^{4}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.6$ to $Δx=0.3,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}.",
  "worked_solution": "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.6) & =6(0.6)^{3}-(0.6)^{4} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.216-1\\cdot 0.129\\,6 \\\\  & =1.296-0.129\\,6 \\\\  & =1.166\\,4 \\\\  & ≈1.166.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.3) & =6(0.3)^{3}-(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.027-1\\cdot 0.008\\,1 \\\\  & =0.162-0.008\\,1 \\\\  & =0.153\\,9 \\\\  & ≈0.154.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.6$ to $0.3.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.6}{0.3}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={2}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $1.166\\,4$ to $0.153\\,9.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{1.166\\,4}{0.153\\,9}=7.578\\,94…≈7.579.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈7.578\\,94… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(7.578\\,94…) \\\\ p & ≈2.922….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$3.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}-(Δx)^{4}\\,⇒\\,e(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{3})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
